<article class="env-definition" id="0BMY">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BMY">Definition <span data-tag="0BMY">58.3.6</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a category and let $F : \mathcal{C} \to \textit{Sets}$ be a functor. The pair $(\mathcal{C}, F)$ is a <em>Galois category</em> if </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$\mathcal{C}$ has finite limits and finite colimits, 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" id="0BMZ"><p>
 every object of $\mathcal{C}$ is a finite (possibly empty) coproduct of connected objects, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$F(X)$ is finite for all $X \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$F$ reflects isomorphisms<a class="footnotemark" href="#a0001189384" id="a0001189384-mark"><sup>2</sup></a> and is exact<a class="footnotemark" href="#a0001189390" id="a0001189390-mark"><sup>3</sup></a>. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Here we say $X \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})$ is connected if it is not initial and for any monomorphism $Y \to X$ either $Y$ is initial or $Y \to X$ is an isomorphism. </p>
</article>